{#
_workspace.html.twig — the active workspace's layout tree, as markup (#755).

    {% import '_layout/_workspace.html.twig' as workspace %}
    {{ workspace.node(layout.active.root, bodies) }}

`bodies` maps a pane kind ('list', 'calendar', …) to that pane's whole markup:
the <section data-layout-pane> and everything in it, rendered once by the
mailbox shell. This file only places them. The page's layout--workspace
controller builds the same structure when the layout changes, and it MOVES
those sections between cells rather than re-rendering them, so a pane's frame,
scroll position and open contact survive a split or a move. Its build is the
reference for the markup below; the two must agree, or the first change after
a load would redraw the page into a slightly different shape.

  .layout-split   one split: data-layout-split is its id (the device's sizes
                  are keyed by it), data-dir its direction
  .layout-cell    one child of a split, carrying the child's share as its
                  flex-grow — the only place a size lives in the DOM
  .layout-divider the boundary between two cells, a window splitter in the
                  ARIA sense

Sizes are this device's (layout.sizes, from the cookie DeviceLayout reads),
falling back to the workspace's own proportions.
#}

{% macro node(node, bodies) %}
    {% import _self as workspace %}
    {% if node.kind is defined %}
        {{ bodies[node.kind.value] }}
    {% else %}
        {% set sizes = layout.sizes(node) %}
        <div class="layout-split" data-layout-split="{{ node.id }}" data-dir="{{ node.direction.value }}">
            {% for child in node.children %}
                {% if not loop.first %}
                    {{ workspace.divider(node) }}
                {% endif %}
                <div class="layout-cell" style="flex-grow: {{ sizes[loop.index0]|round(4) }}">
                    {{ workspace.node(child, bodies) }}
                </div>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
    {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{#
The boundary between two cells. A separator rather than a button: it has a
position and arrow keys, which is what a window splitter is. Its value is not
announced as a number — the share of a box whose size changes with the window
means nothing read aloud — so it carries a label only.
#}
{% macro divider(split) %}
    <div class="layout-divider"
         role="separator"
         tabindex="0"
         aria-orientation="{{ split.direction.value == 'row' ? 'vertical' : 'horizontal' }}"
         aria-label="{{ 'layout.divider'|trans }}"
         title="{{ 'layout.divider_hint'|trans }}"
         data-action="pointerdown->layout--workspace#startResize keydown->layout--workspace#nudge dblclick->layout--workspace#evenOut"></div>
{% endmacro %}

{#
The attributes of one pane's outer <section class="layout-pane">. `kind` is
the PaneKind case; its value names the pane for the controller and the
stylesheet; the two minimums are what a divider
stops at and what a split must leave room for (PaneKind::minWidth(),
minHeight()). The section and its content are the caller's.
#}
{% macro attrs(kind) -%}
    data-layout-pane="{{ kind.value }}" data-min-w="{{ kind.minWidth }}" data-min-h="{{ kind.minHeight }}" aria-label="{{ kind.transKey|trans }}"
{%- endmacro %}
